Let OMNI_T2M_DEVICE override the flow_matching device

token2wav has two stages. The vocoder already honours OMNI_VOC_DEVICE, which
takes precedence over every platform default; flow_matching (token2mel) has no
such escape hatch and is pinned to CPU at compile time whenever GGML_USE_CANN
is defined:

    #ifdef GGML_USE_CANN
        std::string device_token2mel = "cpu";
        print_with_timestamp("Token2Wav: CANN流跨线程需算子适配，flow_matching暂用CPU\n");
    #else
        std::string device_token2mel = token2wav_device;
    #endif

The stated reason is that CANN binds a device context to the calling thread
(aclrtSetDevice, tracked by the thread_local g_current_cann_device), so streams
created by the initialising thread cannot be used from the streaming TTS
consumer thread without aclrtSetCurrentContext, which ggml-cann does not plumb.

That reasoning holds for the duplex streaming path. It does not obviously hold
for batch evaluation, which drives one sample at a time. Setting
OMNI_VOC_DEVICE=gpu:0 on Ascend already puts the vocoder on CANN0 in the
tts_seed pipeline without error, so the blanket #ifdef is at least partly
over-conservative there.

This adds the missing knob so the question can be answered by measurement
rather than by reading the ifdef. The default is unchanged on every platform:
without OMNI_T2M_DEVICE set, CANN builds still pin flow_matching to CPU.

Measured on Ascend 910 with the tts_seed zh pipeline, per sample, all with ACL
graph capture at its default (on) so the rows are comparable:

    both stages on CPU                  153s    RTF 25.6
    vocoder on NPU (OMNI_VOC_DEVICE)    152s    RTF 23.2
    both on NPU (this patch)            5.4s    RTF 1.00

Turning graph capture off is worth another 1.2x on top (RTF 0.83, 4.2s), which
is a separate finding: with token2wav on the NPU this workload is a long chain
of small ops, the shape capture should help most, and it measures as a net loss.

flow_matching is where the cost lives, and the vocoder escape hatch alone
recovers almost none of it. Most of what it holds is per-sample work rather
than a fixed cost: conditioning each sample's reference audio through the flow
model takes 54.6s on the CPU and 2.7s on the NPU, which is why raising
OMNI_T2W_THREADS does not help (8 -> 64 threads buys 6% on a 640-core host —
a long chain of small ops that ggml's CPU backend cannot spread out).

Output is unaffected: WER 1.84% over the first 10 zh samples scored with
paraformer, with the three non-zero scores all homophone substitutions on the
ASR side.

diff --git a/tools/omni/omni.cpp b/tools/omni/omni.cpp
--- a/tools/omni/omni.cpp
+++ b/tools/omni/omni.cpp
@@ -4341,8 +4341,14 @@
             // Device configuration - 使用 omni_init 传入的 token2wav_device 参数
             // 格式: "gpu", "gpu:0", "gpu:1", "cpu"
+            // OMNI_T2M_DEVICE overrides the platform default below, mirroring
+            // what OMNI_VOC_DEVICE does for the vocoder a few lines down.
+            const char * t2m_dev_env = getenv("OMNI_T2M_DEVICE");
 #ifdef GGML_USE_CANN
             std::string device_token2mel = "cpu";
             print_with_timestamp("Token2Wav: CANN流跨线程需算子适配，flow_matching暂用CPU\n");
 #else
             std::string device_token2mel = token2wav_device;
 #endif
+            if (t2m_dev_env && t2m_dev_env[0]) {
+                device_token2mel = t2m_dev_env;
+                print_with_timestamp("Token2Wav: flow_matching device overridden by OMNI_T2M_DEVICE=%s\n", t2m_dev_env);
+            }
